sieve-filter-v2
; Gap fill: after a leap of more than `leap` semitones, the next note turns back the other way by a
; step of at most `step` semitones, as singers and counterpoint expect. Rests are passed over (the
; next note is measured from the last note before them). With `resolve`, a melody may not end on a
; leap still waiting to be filled. The state is nothing yet (0), or the last note's pitch p and
; what the next note owes: 1 + (p - 1) * 3 + owed, owed 0 nothing, 1 a step down, 2 a step up.
id        melody-gapfill
version   1
author    Edward James Gordon (written with Claude)
origin    ai-directed
lines     audio
symbols   notes*
describe  After a leap, the next note steps back the other way.
param     leap     int     4   1  24  a move wider than this many semitones is a leap
param     step     int     2   1  12  the widest step that fills a leap, in semitones
param     resolve  choice  no  no,yes whether a melody may end on an unfilled leap

states    {1 + PITCHES * 3}
start     0
accept    0
for p 1 {PITCHES}
  accept  {1 + (p - 1) * 3}
  if {resolve == 0}
    accept  {1 + (p - 1) * 3 + 1} {1 + (p - 1) * 3 + 2}
  fi
done
for d 0 {DURATIONS - 1}
  t 0 @{d} 0
  for q 1 {PITCHES}
    t 0 @{q * DURATIONS + d} {1 + (q - 1) * 3}
  done
  for p 1 {PITCHES}
    for owed 0 2
      t {1 + (p - 1) * 3 + owed} @{d} {1 + (p - 1) * 3 + owed}
      for q 1 {PITCHES}
        if {owed == 0 || (owed == 1 && q < p && p - q <= step) || (owed == 2 && q > p && q - p <= step)}
          t {1 + (p - 1) * 3 + owed} @{q * DURATIONS + d} {1 + (q - 1) * 3 + (q - p > leap) + (p - q > leap) * 2}
        fi
      done
    done
  done
done
end
